WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.330
The voice that rises when someone cuts you off, and the voice that rises when you see a child being wronged — both are anger.

2
00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:11.940
But one of them makes you smaller, and the other may be what makes you human.

3
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:18.150
So is the person who never gets angry truly mature, or simply past caring about anything?

4
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:30.000
You are waiting at a red light in morning traffic.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.610
The lane to your right is barely moving, someone swerves in front of you without signalling, and comes to a stop twenty centimetres from your front bumper.

6
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:48.340
There is not even time to put a name to what is happening in your body: a heat at the nape of your neck, a locking of the jaw, a force in your hands that grips the wheel harder than it needs to.

7
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:57.910
Before the sound of the horn has died away, three sentences assemble themselves at once inside your head, and even if you speak none of them, they have still been assembled.

8
00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:03.480
Then the light changes, the man drives off, you drive off.

9
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:10.330
Ten minutes later you cannot remember his face, but the tightness in your chest soaks into the whole first half of your day.

10
00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:17.180
That same evening, at the supermarket, a woman at the till is waving a receipt and shouting at the cashier.

11
00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.190
The sum in dispute is seven lira.

12
00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:28.160
The young cashier apologises, the woman cuts the apology off and starts again, and the people in the queue choose to look at their phones.

13
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.610
And you, at exactly that moment, feel the same thing again.

14
00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:36.420
The same heat at the nape, the same lock in the jaw.

15
00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.070
Your body does not distinguish between the two situations.

16
00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:47.400
Your physiology writes the anger that leans on a car horn and the anger that burns at an injustice in the very same alphabet.

17
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.090
This is where the whole problem begins.

18
00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:56.620
Because although these two angers carry an identical bodily signature, morally they are two quite different things.

19
00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:03.150
In the first, what was obstructed was my lane, my forty seconds, my sense of precedence.

20
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:10.800
In the second, what was obstructed was somebody else's dignity, and I notice it while nothing of my own interest has been touched at all.

21
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.810
The first anger reports on me.

22
00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:16.980
The second anger reports on the world.

23
00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.310
Now let us look at the person who never gets angry.

24
00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.800
There is certainly someone like this in your life.

25
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.970
He does not raise his voice.

26
00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.660
He does not get drawn into arguments.

27
00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:33.950
When bad news arrives, not a muscle moves in his face.

28
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:38.080
Everyone who speaks of him reaches for the same word: mature.

29
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.970
Serene.

30
00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:41.860
Settled.

31
00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:48.070
The man stands there like a statue of virtue, and reminds the tense people around him of their own tension.

32
00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:50.760
But let us add one detail.

33
00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:57.930
That same man heard, for eleven years, a child being beaten every evening in the flat next door, and did nothing.

34
00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:02.860
When the sounds on the other side of the wall rose, he turned up the television a little.

35
00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:08.910
He never once knocked on the door, his face never once flushed, he never once lay awake.

36
00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.080
Does the word mature still sit comfortably now?

37
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:19.090
Or does that same serenity have another name: numbness, blindness, the art of never disturbing one's own comfort?

38
00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:23.380
Because to the eye looking in from outside, the two are indistinguishable.

39
00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:30.070
The man who has disciplined himself for years and the man to whom nothing matters wear the same calm face.

40
00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:32.920
They speak in the same soft voice.

41
00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.570
They listen with the same patient gaze.

42
00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:40.860
Serenity is the shared costume of virtue and insensibility.

43
00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.870
Let us try the reverse as well.

44
00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:48.480
Picture a man who gives the waiter a dressing-down because his soup arrived cold.

45
00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:56.930
The same man, a month later, seeing three people dismissed unfairly at his workplace, becomes the only one who strikes the table and objects.

46
00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.620
In the first scene we look down on him.

47
00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:01.670
In the second we admire him.

48
00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:05.800
But the capacity for anger is the same capacity in both.

49
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:10.570
Two ladles of water from the same cauldron.

50
00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:14.540
Which means there is a fault in the way the question has been framed.

51
00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:23.950
When we ask whether the person who gets angry is better or the person who does not, we are asking a question with no answer, because the axis is the wrong axis.

52
00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:30.480
The right question is this: anger at what, how much, when, against whom, and for how long?

53
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.570
And the truly decisive one is the last: what did I do after I became angry?

54
00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:42.580
There are two thinkers who first asked these questions properly, and the two of them give almost exactly opposite answers.

55
00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:46.870
One says anger cannot be disciplined and must be torn out by the root.

56
00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:51.800
The other says a person incapable of anger cannot even be counted as a person.

57
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.410
Seneca wrote his work On Anger at the request of his brother Novatus.

58
00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:06.460
The text is thought to have been composed in the years after the killing of Caligula, that is, in a period when Rome had only just seen that the anger of a single man could become state policy.

59
00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:12.350
Right at the start of the book, Seneca relays that certain wise men had called anger a brief madness.

60
00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:17.920
The Roman poet Horace had put the same idea more sharply: anger is a short insanity.

61
00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.050
Seneca takes this diagnosis and follows it to the end.

62
00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:31.620
For him, looking at the face of an angry man is evidence enough: the eyes widened, the colour drained, the breathing irregular, the hands unsteady.

63
00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.590
These are not the signs of an illness but of a derangement of the mind.

64
00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:43.880
A madman is not ashamed of his madness, and the angry man, in the very moment of his anger, is at his most certain of being in the right.

65
00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.410
That is where the danger lies.

66
00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:57.100
Seneca's real philosophical move comes when he answers this objection: but without anger no one fights a war, without anger no justice is done, without anger nobody lifts a finger.

67
00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.310
Seneca refuses this.

68
00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:04.880
No virtue, he says, needs the assistance of a vice in order to do its work.

69
00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:10.770
A good physician does not get angry with his patient, he treats the disease.

70
00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:16.500
A good judge does not get angry with the offender, he sets the penalty by the measure of the offence.

71
00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:23.350
Indeed quite the opposite: a punishment delivered in anger is always either excessive or lands in the wrong place.

72
00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:29.720
An army, too, fights by discipline and not by rage; the soldier who charges in anger breaks the line.

73
00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:36.730
And then the distinction of Seneca's that fits the modern argument best of all: the first stirring of the body is not yet anger.

74
00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:43.900
That heat you feel at your nape when someone cuts in front of you lies outside our will, and it is not in our power to abolish it.

75
00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:51.230
Anger is born when the mind gives its assent to that stirring, when it says, this is an injustice done to me and it must be answered.

76
00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:56.160
Anger, in other words, is not an emotional accident but a judgement.

77
00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:01.890
Because it is a judgement, it can be mistaken; and because it is a judgement, it can be withdrawn.

78
00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:13.060
This is what the Stoic state, translated as freedom from passion and misunderstood for centuries, actually amounts to: not failing to feel, but refusing to sign off on that first stirring straight away.

79
00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:18.150
Seneca also places in his book a scene that carries the full severity of the matter.

80
00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:23.880
Caligula has the son of Pastor, a man of the Roman equestrian class, put to death.

81
00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:28.970
On the evening of that same day, Caligula invites the father to his table.

82
00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:33.260
Pastor comes, he drinks, he even appears cheerful.

83
00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.630
Because he has another son.

84
00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:45.840
At that table, that evening, there sits a father who does not become angry, and his serenity is not a virtue; it is a calculation, the only bargaining chip a father has left.

85
00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:50.770
Seneca tells this scene in order to show how cruel the tyrant was.

86
00:07:50.770 --> 00:08:00.660
But the scene shows us something else as well: beneath the refusal to be angry there may be virtue, and there may equally be fear, helplessness, calculation.

87
00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:04.630
From outside, all of them are the same calm face.

88
00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:13.560
Aristotle, roughly three hundred years before Seneca, enters the question from precisely the opposite end in the fourth book of the Nicomachean Ethics.

89
00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:21.210
In his account of character, every virtue stands between two extremes, and the virtue belonging to the field of anger is mildness.

90
00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:30.620
This virtue has an excess: the man who boils over at once, who is angry at everything, who does not himself know whom he is angry with.

91
00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:41.310
Aristotle even divides this type into sub-categories: those who flare up and subside immediately, those who store it inside and carry it for years, those who cannot be appeased at all.

92
00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:48.160
But that other word in Aristotle's vocabulary is the key to the whole debate: aorgesia, angerlessness.

93
00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.970
And he classifies it not as a virtue but as a deficiency.

94
00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:57.380
The man who fails to be angry at what ought to anger him, he says, appears to be a fool.

95
00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:04.390
To put up with being insulted, to overlook an insult offered to one's own people, is the mark of a slavish soul.

96
00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:12.680
That sentence does what very few sentences in the history of moral philosophy have done: it sets calmness directly alongside servility.

97
00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:18.730
For Aristotle, being able to feel anger at something is proof that the thing has value for you.

98
00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:26.700
If you are angry at nothing, then either you have transcended everything or you hold nothing of worth; and the second is far more common than the first.

99
00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:39.630
Aristotle's criterion from the second book of the same work belongs here too: getting angry is easy, anyone can get angry; what is difficult is to be angry with the right person, to the right degree, at the right time,

100
00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:43.280
for the right reason and in the right way.

101
00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:49.170
Notice that what is being tested here is not the existence of anger but its address and its calibration.

102
00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:55.380
The difference between the two thinkers is not a difference of temperament but a difference in the design of the human being.

103
00:09:55.380 --> 00:10:01.590
For Seneca the passions are false judgements, and a false judgement is to be uprooted and thrown away.

104
00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:08.280
For Aristotle the passions are the raw material of virtue, and raw material is not thrown away, it is worked.

105
00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:17.210
According to the one, trying to carve anger into shape is like trying to domesticate an explosive: you cannot do it, you only delay the detonation.

106
00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:22.780
According to the other, tearing anger out of a person tears out his sense of justice along with it.

107
00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:29.950
And into the exact middle of this dilemma, from an altogether different geography, falls a sentence just two words long.

108
00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:33.920
There is a scene recorded in the hadith collections.

109
00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:38.050
A man comes to the Prophet and asks for a piece of counsel.

110
00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:42.180
The answer he receives is short: do not become angry.

111
00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:45.510
The man insists and asks for the counsel again.

112
00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:49.960
The same answer comes: do not become angry.

113
00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:54.730
The man asks a third time, and the answer still does not change.

114
00:10:54.730 --> 00:11:01.420
This report, compiled by Bukhari, stands like an entire moral teaching compressed into two words.

115
00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:05.870
Those two words have been read in two distinct ways for centuries.

116
00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:12.240
The first reading is this: suppress the feeling, learn to fall silent, do not stir.

117
00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:17.330
The outcome of that reading arrives precisely at what Aristotle called the slavish soul.

118
00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:26.740
The second reading takes the sentence not as a prohibition but as an instruction in discipline: take hold of the reins of your anger, do not let it drag you, you drive it.

119
00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:31.190
The first piece of evidence for the second reading is the sources themselves.

120
00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:36.440
Because those same sources also record the moments in which the Prophet did become angry.

121
00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:47.450
In an incident related by Bukhari, he sternly rebukes a prayer leader who was wearying the congregation by drawing out the prayer excessively, and demands to know whether he is driving people into hardship.

122
00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:52.860
So anger was not something that had never once occurred in the life of the man giving the counsel.

123
00:11:52.860 --> 00:12:01.790
It had occurred, but it did not fall everywhere; it fell in one particular place: on an unnecessary burden being loaded onto the back of the weak.

124
00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:05.440
The second piece of evidence is hidden inside the language.

125
00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:15.170
In the Qur'an, in the one hundred and thirty-fourth verse of the chapter Al-i Imran, the expression used in describing the praiseworthy human type is kazimin al-ghayz.

126
00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:25.220
The root here means not the one who has no anger but the one who holds his anger in; its dictionary sense has to do with putting a bridle in an animal's mouth, with pulling on its halter.

127
00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:29.510
The verse then immediately adds forgiving others.

128
00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:39.400
The human being the text praises, in other words, is not the one in whom nothing stirs; it is the one who, while something stirs within him, can keep hold of its halter.

129
00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:43.210
A bridle is of no use to someone with no horse.

130
00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:46.540
The third piece of evidence lies in practice.

131
00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:55.470
In a report compiled by Abu Dawud, a person who becomes angry while standing is advised to sit down, and if it does not pass, to lie down.

132
00:12:55.470 --> 00:13:01.840
This is not a denial of the emotion; it is an acknowledgement that the emotion is something that dissolves over time.

133
00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:09.010
For someone to say to you, sit down, is to say at one and the same time that what you feel is real and that it will pass.

134
00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:13.780
The clearest philosophical statement of this tradition comes from al-Ghazali.

135
00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:22.070
In the work known as The Revival of the Religious Sciences, or Ihya for short, he devotes a separate section to anger and puts the matter directly:

136
00:13:22.070 --> 00:13:27.000
those who argue that the faculty of anger must be extinguished altogether are mistaken.

137
00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:36.090
For this faculty was not given to the human being for nothing; protecting oneself, resisting oppression, defending one's dignity are all done by means of it.

138
00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:40.700
What al-Ghazali is after is itidal, that is, correct calibration.

139
00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:46.270
Too much of it makes a man an oppressor, too little makes him content with humiliation.

140
00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:55.680
For him, the person with no anger at all is not virtuous but devoid of any sense of honour; and the absence of that sense is not a loftiness but a defect.

141
00:13:55.680 --> 00:14:05.250
Notice how close al-Ghazali's criterion stands to Aristotle's; one in Baghdad and Tus, the other in Athens, and both pointing at the same middle ground.

142
00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:10.980
Ibn Miskawayh builds the same line more systematically in his work The Refinement of Character.

143
00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:16.070
The virtue corresponding to the soul's faculty of anger is shaja'a, courage.

144
00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:23.720
The excess of this faculty is tahawwur: unmeasured recklessness, an imitation of bravery that burns both its owner and others.

145
00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:27.050
Its deficiency is jubn, cowardice.

146
00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:30.380
Something very fine is being said here.

147
00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:35.790
In this scheme cowardice is not the opposite of anger but anger left incomplete.

148
00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:44.080
Some people, that is, keep silent not because they are calm but because they are afraid; and the outward appearance of the silence is the same in both.

149
00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:53.010
Now, from inside this same tradition, there is a line we all know: he who strikes needs a man without hands, he who curses needs a man without a tongue.

150
00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:59.700
At first hearing, this saying of Yunus Emre's seems to exalt exactly the person Aristotle called slavish.

151
00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:05.110
But the one Yunus is addressing is not the oppressor out there; it is his own self.

152
00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:14.520
That line is not a proposal for the ordering of society but a rule a dervish lays down for his own inner discipline: I will not take the reckoning for what is done to me.

153
00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:22.330
The difference between renouncing your own right and overlooking someone else's right is the mother of every distinction this episode has been resting on.

154
00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:24.540
The first is a sacrifice.

155
00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:27.390
The second is complicity.

156
00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:36.160
Indeed, the same tradition kept in circulation for centuries a saying that likens the one who keeps silent in the face of injustice to a mute devil.

157
00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:43.490
Specialists in hadith do not consider its attribution to the Prophet sound, and cite it not as a hadith but as a saying of wisdom.

158
00:15:43.490 --> 00:15:53.700
But the fact that a saying whose source is disputed has nonetheless remained standing for ten centuries says something in itself: this culture has wanted to know that silence is not always a virtue.

159
00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:59.270
Now take all these criteria and bring them into our own age, and notice something strange.

160
00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:05.800
In the world Seneca, Aristotle and al-Ghazali were arguing about, anger was an expensive thing.

161
00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:13.930
To be angry meant looking into the eyes of the person in front of you, hearing their voice, receiving their answer, and sometimes paying the price.

162
00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:17.580
Because anger had a cost, where it was spent mattered.

163
00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:21.550
Today anger is cheaper than it has ever been in human history.

164
00:16:21.550 --> 00:16:24.880
Writing a sentence takes nine seconds.

165
00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:28.050
The other party is not a face but a name.

166
00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:33.940
There is no cost, and in fact there is a reward: likes, shares, approval.

167
00:16:33.940 --> 00:16:37.590
The economy of anger has been turned inside out.

168
00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:41.880
It used to be expensive to be angry; now it pays.

169
00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:45.850
There is a measurable side to this transformation as well.

170
00:16:45.850 --> 00:16:55.420
A study published in two thousand seventeen in the journal of the National Academy of Sciences in the United States found that in political messages shared on social media,

171
00:16:55.420 --> 00:17:00.030
each moral-emotional word raised the rate of diffusion by roughly twenty percent.

172
00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:05.920
The logic of the platform, in other words, carries the angry sentence faster than the calm one.

173
00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:14.690
In the same year, the neuroscientist Molly Crockett argued that the digital environment lowers the cost of expressing moral outrage while raising its reward,

174
00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:18.980
and that this can turn outrage from a moral response into a habit.

175
00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:21.510
Habit is an important word.

176
00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:29.320
Because habit is the word on which Aristotle's entire ethical system rests; virtue is acquired by habit, and so is vice.

177
00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:34.090
So let us apply Aristotle's five criteria to the anger of the present.

178
00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:36.620
At the right person?

179
00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:45.390
Most of the time, no; anger falls less on the guilty party than on whoever stands nearest to them, whoever is most reachable, most defenceless.

180
00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:47.920
To the right degree?

181
00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:54.610
There is no scale; words of the same intensity are spent on a parking dispute and on the ruin of a country.

182
00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:57.140
For the right reason?

183
00:17:57.140 --> 00:18:02.710
Sometimes, but another reason is always mixed in alongside the first: being seen.

184
00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:06.680
And the real question, for the right length of time?

185
00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:10.170
Here there is almost nothing that passes the test.

186
00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:18.140
An injustice lasts two days, on the third day it gives way to a new injustice, and a week later nobody can recall the victim's name.

187
00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:28.990
Has the person who carried their anger for forty-eight hours and then set it down really been angry at that injustice, or have they merely consumed the brief sensation of moral goodness that being angry provides?

188
00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:38.560
At this point contemporary moral philosophy splits cleanly in two, and the interesting thing is that the split almost coincides with the line Seneca and Aristotle drew.

189
00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:47.490
On one side there is Martha Nussbaum; in her study Anger and Forgiveness she underlines a wish buried inside anger: the wish that the other party suffer.

190
00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:53.700
And she asks: does the suffering of the perpetrator bring back what the victim lost?

191
00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:59.590
If it does not, then that portion of anger is not moral energy but no more than a fantasy.

192
00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:06.440
This is why Nussbaum says the only useful form of anger is the form that turns its gaze from punishment to repair.

193
00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:11.530
The echo, twenty centuries later, of Seneca's analogy of the physician.

194
00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:15.500
On the other side there are thinkers such as Myisha Cherry.

195
00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:23.470
Cherry marks off a kind of anger that she names after a poet and writer: anger whose target is not the fall of a person but the breaking of an order.

196
00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:27.280
This anger, she says, carries information.

197
00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:34.290
It tells you what is unbearable, where a limit has been trampled, and no other emotion brings that news as quickly.

198
00:19:34.290 --> 00:19:41.140
A society that has entirely given up being angry at injustice loses the organ by which injustice is noticed.

199
00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:47.190
The echo, twenty-four centuries later, of Aristotle's diagnosis of the slavish soul.

200
00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:56.280
And there is a single criterion binding these two sides together, in which all the threads accumulating since the beginning of this episode gather: the day after your anger.

201
00:19:56.280 --> 00:20:02.650
Because anger in itself is neither a virtue nor a vice; it is a tension, an energy, an alarm.

202
00:20:02.650 --> 00:20:07.100
An alarm does not save a building; it saves it if it gets the people out.

203
00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:15.710
So the answer to the question of whether the person who never gets angry is virtuous or insensible lies not in that person's face but in their next day.

204
00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:27.840
The man who heard the child behind the wall stayed calm, and in the morning went and knocked on the door, then notified the people who needed to be notified, then followed it up for a month: his serenity is a virtue,

205
00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:31.650
indeed a virtue that has done more than anger could have done.

206
00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:37.540
The same man stayed calm and did nothing: that serenity is not virtue but comfort.

207
00:20:37.540 --> 00:20:44.230
And what separates these two is not the absence or presence of the feeling but the presence or absence of the act.

208
00:20:44.230 --> 00:20:49.640
The same criterion tests the angry person too, and there it stings a great deal more.

209
00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:56.490
The one who struck the table, who raised his voice, who was in the right: did he do anything the next day?

210
00:20:56.490 --> 00:21:01.260
Did he write a petition, call one person, take on one risk?

211
00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:11.950
If he did not, then that anger was not justice but a discharge; worse, because it gave him the sensation of having done justice, it was the very thing that kept him from actually doing it.

212
00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:18.800
Seneca's calm and Aristotle's anger meet in the same examination: neither counts as a virtue until it turns into work.

213
00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:24.050
Perhaps the last question to ask is this: who pays the bill for your anger?

214
00:21:24.050 --> 00:21:30.260
When you lose your temper at the man who cuts in front of you in traffic, you pay the bill, with half your day.

215
00:21:30.260 --> 00:21:36.950
When you are angry at the woman shouting at the till and say nothing, that young cashier pays the bill, by being left alone.

216
00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:45.080
And when you turn your anger into work at the place where you saw the injustice, the bill goes to the right address for the first time: to the one who committed it.

217
00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:48.730
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

218
00:21:48.730 --> 00:21:52.060
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
